│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ structured_seeds_gradient_anneal                                                          │
│ patch_description        │ Two key problems in the current code: (1) the "two staggered heptagons" seeds were        │
│                          │ appended as 7 separate (2,3) arrays, which the shape-check silently discarded — so almost │
│                          │ all structured seeds were wasted; (2) the annealer only made random single-point          │
│                          │ perturbations, which is very slow at climbing the spread (dmin/dmax) landscape. This edit │
│                          │ (a) fixes/extends the seed set with expert geometric constructions for the 14-point       │
│                          │ spread problem in R^3 — a hexagonal antiprism with two poles (analytically ~0.473 ratio), │
│                          │ an icosahedron with two antipodal vertices split into point pairs (a perturbation of the  │
│                          │ optimal 12-point icosahedral configuration, ratio 0.526), a rhombic dodecahedron, a       │
│                          │ cuboctahedron plus axial points, and heptagon ring variants — and (b) replaces the        │
│                          │ random-walk local search with a gradient-style annealer: each step it pushes apart all    │
│                          │ pairs near the current minimum distance, mildly pulls together pairs at the diameter      │
│                          │ (shrinking dmax), adds decaying random jitter, then renormalizes the diameter to 1. This  │
│                          │ directly ascends the spread objective and should raise the ratio from ~0.39 toward the    │
│                          │ ~0.49 reference.                                                                          │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
