│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ add_ring_seeds_and_polish_restarts                                                        │
│ patch_description        │ The current score already matches the reference (combined_score ≈ 1.00), so any gain      │
│                          │ requires finding a configuration with squared ratio strictly above 1/4.1658 ≈ 0.2400. Two │
│                          │ targeted improvements:                                                                    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. Better structured seeds. For n=14 on/around a sphere, a very strong candidate family   │
│                          │ is the "two staggered hexagonal rings + two poles" geometry (related to the known best    │
│                          │ Tammes-like arrangements for 14 points, e.g. staggered rings give many nearly-equal       │
│                          │ contacts). Two ring variants (with/without 30° offset and varied height) plus the         │
│                          │ existing icosahedron/cube starts broaden the basin coverage. Following the batch          │
│                          │ recommendation, I add an explicit shape assertion after building each seed so a           │
│                          │ truncation bug fails loudly instead of silently returning a wrong-size array.             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. Stronger final polish. The single exact-ratio L-BFGS-B polish often stalls at a kink   │
│                          │ of the min/max ratio (the objective is nonsmooth when the argmin/argmax pair switches).   │
│                          │ Running several polish restarts, each from the incumbent plus a tiny random jitter, and   │
│                          │ keeping the best result, reliably squeezes out extra ratio. This is cheap (the exact      │
│                          │ gradient is O(n²)) and keeps total runtime well under the previous 71s outlier.           │
│ num_applied              │ 2                                                                                         │
