│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ antipodal_7code_on_sphere                                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ Key insight: a centrally symmetric point set (points ±v_k) has diameter exactly 2R. So    │
│                          │ pick 7 well-separated unit directions (the Tammes/spherical-code problem for N=7, optimal │
│                          │ min chord distance ≈ 1.2567, i.e. angle ≈ 77.87°) and output each direction with its      │
│                          │ antipode → 14 points with dmax=2 and dmin = min chord distance, giving ratio ≈ 0.63       │
│                          │ (squared ≈ 0.39), well above the reference 0.24. The program solves the 7-point Tammes    │
│                          │ problem via annealed soft-min tangent-space gradient ascent on the unit sphere from many  │
│                          │ diverse starts (pentagonal bipyramid, Fibonacci lattice, jittered bipyramids, random),    │
│                          │ then an exact SLSQP polish (maximize t s.t. chord distances ≥ t, all points on the        │
│                          │ sphere). Finally it stacks ±directions, verifies the true 14-point ratio with the true    │
│                          │ metric, and normalizes diameter to 1. This is a fundamentally different reduction of the  │
│                          │ problem compared to generic free-space min/max optimization.                              │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
