                        Patch Metadata - Gen 43/200 - Novelty: 1/3 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 2/3                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ constrained_slsqp_polish_top_candidates                                                   │
│ patch_description        │ The previous edit failed because of an indentation mismatch in the first SEARCH block. I  │
│                          │ retry with the exact verbatim text (module-level functions use 4-space indentation, and   │
│                          │ the trailing helper insertion point differs).                                             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The substantive idea is unchanged: the current program reaches ≈0.96 using only           │
│                          │ stochastic annealing (`_local_search`), which plateaus around dmin/dmax ≈ 0.48 because    │
│                          │ decaying jitter repeatedly knocks points off the active constraints. The prior best       │
│                          │ generation (score 1.00) used a constrained SLSQP polish: maximize t = dmin² subject to    │
│                          │ d²ᵢⱼ ≥ t and d²ᵢⱼ ≤ 1 (after diameter normalization). This is the exact KKT form of the   │
│                          │ problem — at the optimum, minimum-distance pairs sit at the boundary of the diameter      │
│                          │ constraints, which a constrained solver resolves precisely and stochastic search cannot.  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Changes:                                                                                  │
│                          │ 1. Add a `_ratio_sq` helper computing (dmin/dmax)² directly.                              │
│                          │ 2. Collect the top-3 distinct annealing candidates instead of only the best (the          │
│                          │ landscape has many near-tied basins, and the constrained polish may lift any of them past │
│                          │ the best).                                                                                │
│                          │ 3. For each top-3 candidate, apply σ ∈ {0, 0.05, 0.15, 0.3} symmetry-breaking             │
│                          │ perturbations and run SLSQP with per-pair closure-built constraint Jacobians (built in a  │
│                          │ plain loop — the vectorized Jacobian fill from an earlier generation crashed on a shape   │
│                          │ mismatch, so fancy indexing is deliberately avoided).                                     │
│                          │ 4. Trim annealing budgets slightly (2200→1600, 1200→1000, final polish 4000→1500) to keep │
│                          │ total runtime bounded while adding the fast SLSQP stage.                                  │
│ num_applied              │ 2                                                                                         │
